  "sentences": [
    {
      "id": "s1",
      "english": "A voltage-controlled oscillator (VCO) changes its output frequency according to its control voltage.",
      "term_ids": [
        "t-vco",
        "t-frequency",
        "t-control"
      ],
      "literals": []
    },
    {
      "id": "s2",
      "english": "In direct frequency modulation (FM), the message signal drives the VCO's control input.",
      "term_ids": [
        "t-vco",
        "t-fm",
        "t-message"
      ],
      "literals": []
    },
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "english": "In an ideal linear model, f_i(t) = f_c + K_v m(t), so the carrier's instantaneous frequency varies while its amplitude remains constant.",
      "term_ids": [
        "t-instantaneous",
        "t-amplitude"
      ],
      "literals": [
        {
          "text": "f_i(t) = f_c + K_v m(t)",
          "spoken": "f i of t equals f c plus K v times m of t"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "s4",
      "english": "Here f_c is the carrier frequency, K_v is the VCO sensitivity in Hz/V, and m(t) is the message voltage.",
      "term_ids": [
        "t-vco",
        "t-carrier"
      ],
      "literals": [
        {
          "text": "f_c",
          "spoken": "f c"
        },
        {
          "text": "K_v",
          "spoken": "K v"
        },
        {
          "text": "Hz/V",
          "spoken": "hertz per volt"
        },
        {
          "text": "m(t)",
          "spoken": "m of t"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "s5",
      "english": "For example, in this ideal model, let f_c = 1000 Hz, K_v = 100 Hz/V, and m(t) = 0.5 V.",
      "literals": [
        {
          "text": "f_c = 1000 Hz",
          "spoken": "f c equals one thousand hertz"
        },
        {
          "text": "K_v = 100 Hz/V",
          "spoken": "K v equals one hundred hertz per volt"
        },
        {
          "text": "m(t) = 0.5 V",
          "spoken": "m of t equals zero point five volts"
        }
      ],
      "term_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "s6",
      "english": "Substituting these values gives f_i(t) = 1050 Hz, an increase of 50 Hz above f_c.",
      "literals": [
        {
          "text": "f_i(t) = 1050 Hz",
          "spoken": "f i of t equals one thousand fifty hertz"
        },
        {
          "text": "50 Hz",
          "spoken": "fifty hertz"
        },
        {
          "text": "f_c",
          "spoken": "f c"
        }
      ],
      "term_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "s7",
      "english": "If m(t) = -0.5 V instead, the same ideal model gives f_i(t) = 950 Hz, a decrease of 50 Hz below f_c.",
      "literals": [
        {
          "text": "m(t) = -0.5 V",
          "spoken": "m of t equals minus zero point five volts"
        },
        {
          "text": "f_i(t) = 950 Hz",
          "spoken": "f i of t equals nine hundred fifty hertz"
        },
        {
          "text": "50 Hz",
          "spoken": "fifty hertz"
        },
        {
          "text": "f_c",
          "spoken": "f c"
        }
      ],
      "term_ids": []
    },
    {
      "id": "s8",
      "english": "These examples describe the assumed ideal relationship, not a guarantee that every real VCO has a perfectly linear response or constant amplitude over its entire operating range.",
      "literals": [],
      "term_ids": [
        "t-vco",
        "t-amplitude"
      ]
    }
  ]
}
